# Ruby on Rails patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' design guidance to idiomatic Rails design and its anti-patterns. Applies to `*.rb` and `*.erb` files.

## MVC boundaries

- A controller action reads params, calls a model/service, and renders/redirects; no inline business logic (validation rules, multi-step workflows, external API calls).
- Move non-trivial conditional or data-shaping logic out of a view into a helper or presenter/decorator.
- A model keeps persistence concerns (validations, associations, scopes) and behavior of the record itself; extract callbacks that orchestrate unrelated side effects (email, third-party API, another model) into a service object or an `ActiveJob`.

## Concerns

- A `Concern` (`app/models/concerns`, `app/controllers/concerns`) bundles cohesive behavior shared by more than one class; if nothing else will `include` it, it is the class's own method.
- Name a concern for the capability it adds (`Archivable`, `Sluggable`), not the model it was extracted from.

## Service objects

- Extract a plain Ruby service object (`SomethingVerb`, one public entry point, e.g. `.call`) when logic spans multiple models, calls an external service, or needs its own tests; match the app's existing convention (directory, base class, `.call` vs `#call`).
- Keep framework concerns (params, session, rendering) out of a service object; pass plain values or model instances.

## ActiveRecord query patterns

- Eager-load before iterating (`Book.includes(:author)`); prefer `includes` over hand-choosing `preload`/`eager_load` unless one is needed.
- Push filtering/aggregation into the database (`where`, `group`, `count`, a named `scope`), not Ruby-side.
- Use a named `scope` for a query fragment reused in more than one place.

## Background jobs

- Make an `ActiveJob` idempotent (queue adapters are at-least-once): guard with a uniqueness check, an idempotency key or an upsert.
- Pass an id into `perform`, not an ActiveRecord object; re-fetch and handle a missing record.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A fat controller action with inline business logic, or a fat model whose callbacks orchestrate unrelated side effects.
- A concern used only to shrink one class's file.
- Raw SQL string interpolation instead of parameterized queries or `sanitize_sql` (see `rules/security.mdc`).
- A `default_scope`; prefer an explicit named scope callers opt into.
